Approach 2:
from collections import deque

class HitCounter:
    def __init__(self):
        self.q = deque()
    
    def hit(self, timestamp):
        self.q.append(timestamp)
    
    def getHits(self, timestamp):
        while self.q and self.q[0] < timestamp - 300:
            self.q.popleft()
        return len(self.q)


Approach 3;
class HitCounter:
    def __init__(self):
        self.bucket = [0] * 300
        self.times = [0] * 300
    
    def hit(self, timestamp):
        index = timestamp % 300
        if self.times[index] != timestamp:
            # New time slot - reset
            self.times[index] = timestamp
            self.bucket[index] = 1
        else:
            # Same time slot - increment
            self.bucket[index] += 1
    
    def getHits(self, timestamp):
        total = 0
        for i in range(300):
            if timestamp - self.times[i] < 300:
                total += self.bucket[i]
        return total


How to Say:
"The key insight of bucketing is that we only care about the last 300 seconds. So instead of storing every individual timestamp, 
I use a circular array of size 300 where each slot represents one second. The index is timestamp mod 300. If the stored time at that slot matches the current timestamp,
I increment the count. If it doesn't match, it means this slot is from a previous cycle so I reset it. GetHits just loops through all 300 slots
and sums counts from valid time slots. This gives O(1) time and O(1) space regardless of traffic volume."



Intutaiation:
timestamp 1   → index 1   (1 % 300 = 1)
timestamp 2   → index 2
timestamp 300 → index 0   (300 % 300 = 0)
timestamp 301 → index 1   (301 % 300 = 1)  ← reuses slot 1
timestamp 302 → index 2
timestamp 600 → index 0   (600 % 300 = 0)  ← reuses slot 0 again



